  "content": {
    "id": "p38-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "If $\\triangle ABC$ has side lengths $a$, $b$ and $c$, prove that:\n$$\\frac{2\\times(\\text{Area of }\\triangle ABC)}{abc}=\\frac{\\sin C}{c}=\\frac{\\sin A}{a}=\\frac{\\sin B}{b}.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{2\\Delta}{abc}=\\dfrac{\\sin C}{c}=\\dfrac{\\sin A}{a}=\\dfrac{\\sin B}{b}$.",
      "worked": "Let $\\Delta$ be the area of $\\triangle ABC$. The area formula with each included angle gives\n$$2\\Delta=ab\\sin C=bc\\sin A=ca\\sin B.$$\nAll three side lengths are positive. Dividing by $abc$ yields\n$$\\frac{2\\Delta}{abc}=\\frac{\\sin C}{c}=\\frac{\\sin A}{a}=\\frac{\\sin B}{b}.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
